{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## DECISION RECORDED — plan-review lane: NEW criterion (operator's explicit refinement), advisory\n\nOperator approved the plan-review guard but directed a **new, separate criterion** rather than extending `removal_rationale` (which fires ONLY on removal/weakening). Verbatim intent: *\"Rather than trying to shoehorn a different criteria and change the trigger, it may make sense to add an additional criteria that requires any alteration of failure/timeout/exception semantics to state its failure-disposition contract.\"* This supersedes this ticket's original \"portability / uncredentialed terminal arm\" framing (that concern is subsumed as one instance below). READY-TO-FIX.\n\n**Add a NEW plan-review criterion — \"failure-disposition contract\"** — authored as its own reviewer overlay + registered in `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json`, with an eval spec, and documented per `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` + `rebar explain <criterion-id>`. **Advisory** posture. Do NOT modify `removal_rationale`'s trigger.\n\n**Bright-line trigger (a disjunction, like T5b/removal_rationale — no subjective judgment):** the plan ADDS or ALTERS failure / timeout / exception / retry / fallback / circuit-breaker semantics on any path (including adding a fallback arm, changing an exception type, changing what disposition a path surfaces, or changing retry/backoff behavior).\n\n**Requirement (what the plan must state to PASS):** its **failure-disposition contract** —\n1. what disposition (retryable/transient vs fatal/permanent) surfaces on each affected failure path; and\n2. when a SECONDARY/fallback path itself fails, WHICH disposition wins — the contract must preserve the most-recoverable leg's disposition, so a retryable primary is never masked by a fallback's own non-retryable failure (the 1c0d root cause, at plan time).\n\n**Subsumes** this ticket's original concern: \"adding a fallback arm whose terminal is uncredentialed in the target env\" is just one instance the contract must address (\"when the fallback arm is unavailable here, what surfaces?\").\n\n**Why this is the highest-leverage guard:** it's the ONLY prevention that fires on the actual origin — `8fbd` was a CONFIG change (\"enable the Anthropic fallback\"); a plan for it would have had to state what happens when that fallback itself fails. Complements the code-review `failover` overlay (c180), which covers code-level changes but not pure config flips. Detection is plan-level contract reasoning (no third-party control-flow reading), which plan-review already does well (T5b precedent).\n\n**Relationship to T5b:** T5b asks whether error-handling/failover EXISTS (retry/backoff/graceful-degradation present, errors surfaced not swallowed). This new criterion is narrower and orthogonal: given a failure path is touched, is its DISPOSITION CONTRACT stated and disposition-preserving? Author it to avoid double-flagging with T5b (cite the disposition-contract gap specifically)."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788061769862343485,"uuid":"0f093e73-03d2-4dc1-9a48-1c20859e81c6"}